From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] mmc: sunxi: report what the block layer actually hands the IDMAC

The descriptor count follows from max_seg_size, which is an upper bound the
driver advertises; what the block layer produces depends on how the pages fall,
and for direct I/O over scattered pages it is usually one 4 KiB segment per
page regardless of what the driver allows.

The difference matters. If segments are 4 KiB in practice, the driver already
fetches as often as the vendor stack does, the "128 against 256 descriptors per
MiB" contrast comes from the calculation, and buf_size never takes the zero
value that Table 11-5 documents as "skip this descriptor". If they are 8 KiB,
both of those hold. This is worth one line of measurement.

So log it: segment count, smallest and largest segment, how many sit exactly at
max_len, and the transfer size, once per request. Silent by default and one
boot argument away, like the other diagnostics in this driver:

    dyndbg="func sunxi_mmc_init_idma_des +p"

Guarded by IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_DYNAMIC_DEBUG) so the loop disappears entirely
where dynamic debug is not built in.

Assisted-by: Claude
---
--- a/drivers/mmc/host/sunxi-mmc.c
+++ b/drivers/mmc/host/sunxi-mmc.c
@@ -431,6 +431,31 @@
 				    host->cfg->idma_des_shift);
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * What the block layer actually handed us, as opposed to the maximum
+	 * we advertised. Descriptor count and segment size decide how often
+	 * the IDMAC has to fetch from memory mid-transfer, and that is one of
+	 * the few things that can leave gaps in the card-side data stream.
+	 * Silent unless asked for: dyndbg="func sunxi_mmc_init_idma_des +p".
+	 */
+	if (IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_DYNAMIC_DEBUG)) {
+		unsigned int lo = UINT_MAX, hi = 0, atmax = 0;
+
+		for (i = 0; i < data->sg_len; i++) {
+			unsigned int len = data->sg[i].length;
+
+			lo = min(lo, len);
+			hi = max(hi, len);
+			if (len == max_len)
+				atmax++;
+		}
+		dev_dbg(mmc_dev(host->mmc),
+			"idma: %u segs, %u..%u bytes, %u at max_len %d, %u bytes total\n",
+			data->sg_len, lo, hi, atmax, max_len,
+			data->blocks * data->blksz);
+		i = data->sg_len;
+	}
+
 	pdes[0].config |= cpu_to_le32(SDXC_IDMAC_DES0_FD);
 	pdes[i - 1].config |= cpu_to_le32(SDXC_IDMAC_DES0_LD |
 					  SDXC_IDMAC_DES0_ER);
